#!/usr/bin/bash
# ==============================================================================
# aquarius-resolve-launch — the front door for DaVinci Resolve
# ==============================================================================
# PLAIN ENGLISH
#
# Resolve does not run on AquariusOS directly. It lives inside a Rocky Linux
# container that `aq resolve install` builds, and it is started by stepping into
# that container and running it there.
#
# This script sits in front of that and hands the container a few things it
# cannot work out for itself.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL — the mouse pointer
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reported from the bench on the old line, 2026-08-28: "the mouse cursor inside
# Resolve changes theme and becomes much smaller than the desktop cursor."
#
# That is not Resolve being odd. It is the whole story of what a container is.
# Resolve is an X11 application, and an X11 application decides what to draw for
# a pointer by reading two settings — XCURSOR_THEME and XCURSOR_SIZE — and then
# looking for that theme ON ITS OWN DISK. Inside the container:
#
#   * neither setting is there, because the container never saw your desktop's
#     settings; and
#   * your cursor theme is not installed in there either, because it is a
#     minimal Rocky Linux with Resolve's libraries and nothing else.
#
# So X falls back to its ancient built-in pointer at its ancient default size,
# and you get a small black arrow in the middle of your own desktop. Every
# containerised application has this problem and most people assume the
# application is just ugly.
#
# THE FIX, IN FOUR PARTS
#   XCURSOR_THEME   read out of YOUR desktop settings, right now, at launch —
#                   not baked in when Resolve was installed, so changing your
#                   cursor theme just works with nothing to re-run.
#   XCURSOR_SIZE    the desktop size and display scale, independent of app scale.
#   XCURSOR_PATH    where to FIND that theme from inside the container.
#                   distrobox mounts the whole host at /run/host, so the themes
#                   on your real disk are visible there.
#   the theme itself is also installed inside the runtime image, so that a
#                   machine using the default pointer works even if the mount
#                   is not where we expect it.
#
# ==============================================================================
# AND THE SECOND HALF: HOW BIG RESOLVE IS
# ==============================================================================
# Reported from the bench, 2026-09-04: "apps resolution and appearing smaller".
#
# THE REASON, and it is the same reason as the pointer. Resolve is an X11
# application. On a Wayland desktop it runs through XWayland, and XWayland hands
# X11 applications a screen with no scale on it at all — always 96 dots per
# inch, always 100%. So a desktop running at 125% or 150% draws everything
# correctly EXCEPT the X11 programs, which come out at their raw size and look
# small beside everything else. GNOME and KDE have exactly the same problem;
# no desktop solves it, because there is nothing in X11 to solve it with.
#
# What CAN be done is to tell the toolkit inside the application. Resolve is
# built on Qt 5, which reads its scale out of the environment, so the launcher
# reads the size your desktop is set to and passes it in. That is the whole
# feature: Resolve follows the desktop, with a 125% minimum when every known
# enabled screen is at least 3200x1800 and is running at 100%. This grows the
# whole interface; it does not alter a clip's proportions or its export size.
#
# ⚠️ WHICH VARIABLE — this is the part that is a judgement, so it is written
#    down rather than assumed:
#
#      QT_SCALE_FACTOR          what we set. Qt's own current variable, it takes
#                               fractional numbers (1.25, 1.5), and it is the
#                               one still documented by Qt.
#      QT_AUTO_SCREEN_SCALE_FACTOR=0
#                               also set, and it matters: left on, Qt asks the
#                               screen how dense it is, and XWayland always
#                               answers "96 dpi", so Qt would confidently undo
#                               the line above.
#      QT_DEVICE_PIXEL_RATIO    the OTHER one, and the one most DaVinci Resolve
#                               posts on the internet recommend. It is Qt 5's
#                               older, deprecated mechanism and it prefers whole
#                               numbers — non-integer values are reported to make
#                               images blocky.
#
#    ⚠️ THE TWO ARE NOT SET TOGETHER, ON PURPOSE. Qt 5 reads both, and where
#       both are set they can multiply — 1.25 and 1.25 becoming 1.56 — which
#       looks like the scaling being broken rather than being doubled. So one at
#       a time, ours by default, and the other reachable in one line for the
#       bench to compare:
#
#           aq resolve scale 1.5                          set the size
#           echo 'qt_variable=device-pixel-ratio' >> ~/.config/aquarius/resolve.conf
#
# WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM, in order:
#   1. AQUARIUS_RESOLVE_SCALE in the environment — a one-off, for getting out of
#      trouble.
#   2. `aq resolve scale N`, saved in ~/.config/aquarius/resolve.conf — for when
#      you want Resolve at a different size from everything else.
#   3. The size this session is running at, asked of aquarius-display-scale,
#      which is the same answer `aq display` gives.
#   4. 1. Which is what every other Linux does, and what looked too small.
#
# Resolve's OWN setting — Preferences → User → UI Settings → Interface Scale —
# still exists and still wins for the parts of Resolve that use it. It is the
# better tool for fine adjustment; this is what makes the first launch right.
#
# USAGE
#   aquarius-resolve-launch [program-inside-the-container] [arguments…]
#   aquarius-resolve-launch [a file to open]
# With no arguments it starts Resolve. The app-menu entries are pointed at this
# script by /usr/libexec/aquarius-resolve-install.
#
# An argument beginning /opt/resolve/ is a program inside the container — that
# is how the Blackmagic RAW Player is started from the same machinery. Anything
# else is a FILE to open, and Resolve is named in front of it. `file://…` URIs
# are turned into plain paths, because that is what Files hands over and Resolve
# does not understand a URI. See the block above the `exec` at the bottom.
#
# It also steps into the folder Blackmagic wants the program started from —
# /opt/resolve, or its own folder for the two RAW tools — INSIDE the container.
# That used to be written on the app-menu entry as `Path=/opt/resolve/`, which
# made GNOME refuse to start Resolve at all, because that folder is not on this
# computer. Full story at "WHICH FOLDER RESOLVE STARTS IN", near the bottom.
#
#   aquarius-resolve-launch --report
# Works out the size, the pointer and which file picker Resolve will get, prints
# the one line it would print on a real launch, and STARTS NOTHING. Added
# 2026-09-08 so that the last step of every install and update — "Matching
# Resolve to your screen" — can ask this script what it would do instead of
# assuming, and so that the image build can ask the same question on a machine
# with no screen and no Resolve.
# ==============================================================================

set -uo pipefail

# --report is handled before anything else, including the "is Resolve set up?"
# question below: reporting what size would be used is a fair question on a
# machine where Resolve is not installed at all, which is exactly what a build
# container is.
REPORT_ONLY=0
if [ "${1:-}" = "--report" ] || [ "${1:-}" = "--dry-run" ]; then
    REPORT_ONLY=1
    shift
fi

RUNTIME_ENV=/usr/share/aquarius/resolve/runtime.env
CONTAINER="aquarius-resolve"
if [ -r "${RUNTIME_ENV}" ]; then
    # shellcheck source=/dev/null
    . "${RUNTIME_ENV}"
    CONTAINER="${AQ_RESOLVE_CONTAINER:-aquarius-resolve}"
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Is it installed?
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A message, not a silent nothing. This script is reached by clicking an icon,
# and an icon that does nothing when clicked is the worst outcome there is.
if [ "${REPORT_ONLY}" = "0" ] \
    && { ! command -v distrobox > /dev/null 2>&1 \
        || ! podman container exists "${CONTAINER}" 2> /dev/null; }; then
    msg="DaVinci Resolve is not set up on this computer yet.

Open your apps and click \"Install DaVinci Resolve\", or run
    aq resolve install
in a terminal."
    if [ -n "${DISPLAY:-}${WAYLAND_DISPLAY:-}" ] && command -v zenity > /dev/null 2>&1; then
        zenity --error --title="DaVinci Resolve" --width=520 --text="${msg}" > /dev/null 2>&1
    fi
    printf '%s\n' "${msg}" >&2
    exit 1
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What does this person's pointer look like?
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GNOME and the Aquarius Desktop both keep it in the same place — the GSettings
# key org.gnome.desktop.interface — so one question answers it for both of the
# sessions AquariusOS ships.
#
# An empty answer is not an error. On a brand-new account the key has never been
# written and gsettings correctly returns the schema default, which is Adwaita
# at 24 — exactly what we would fall back to anyway.
read_setting() { # read_setting <key>
    local v=""
    if command -v gsettings > /dev/null 2>&1; then
        v="$(gsettings get org.gnome.desktop.interface "$1" 2> /dev/null)"
    fi
    # gsettings quotes strings; strip the quotes and the uint32 prefix it puts
    # on numbers.
    v="${v#uint32 }"
    v="${v#\'}"
    v="${v%\'}"
    printf '%s' "${v}"
}

CURSOR_THEME="${XCURSOR_THEME:-$(read_setting cursor-theme)}"
CURSOR_SIZE="${XCURSOR_SIZE:-$(read_setting cursor-size)}"
[ -n "${CURSOR_THEME}" ] || CURSOR_THEME="Adwaita"
[ -n "${CURSOR_SIZE}" ] || CURSOR_SIZE="24"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How big is this screen drawing things?
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Asked of the same helper `aq display` asks, so that Resolve and the rest of
# the desktop can never disagree about the answer. It always prints a number —
# it falls back through your own setting, then GNOME's, then 1 — so an empty
# answer here means the helper is missing, not that the question is unanswerable.
CONF="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-${HOME}/.config}/aquarius/resolve.conf"

conf_value() { # conf_value <key>
    [ -r "${CONF}" ] || return 0
    sed -n "s/^[[:space:]]*$1[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*\([^#]*\).*/\1/p" "${CONF}" \
        | tail -n 1 | tr -d '[:space:]'
}

session_scale() {
    local n=""
    if [ -x /usr/libexec/aquarius-display-scale ]; then
        n="$(/usr/libexec/aquarius-display-scale --resolve-scale 2> /dev/null)"
    fi
    case "${n}" in
        '' | *[!0-9.]* | *.*.*) printf '1' ;;
        *) printf '%s' "${n}" ;;
    esac
}

SCALE="${AQUARIUS_RESOLVE_SCALE:-}"
[ -n "${SCALE}" ] || SCALE="$(conf_value scale)"
[ -n "${SCALE}" ] || SCALE="$(session_scale)"
case "${SCALE}" in
    '' | *[!0-9.]* | *.*.*) SCALE="1" ;;
esac

# XWayland cursor pixels follow the DESKTOP display scale, not Resolve's
# independent interface zoom. On a 1x desktop, 125% Resolve still needs the
# same 24px pointer as other apps (the former calculation incorrectly used 30).
CURSOR_DISPLAY_SCALE=1
if [ -x /usr/libexec/aquarius-display-scale ]; then
    CURSOR_DISPLAY_SCALE="$(/usr/libexec/aquarius-display-scale --cursor-scale 2>/dev/null)"
fi
case "${CURSOR_DISPLAY_SCALE}" in
    ''|*[!0-9.]*|*.*.*) CURSOR_DISPLAY_SCALE=1 ;;
esac
CURSOR_SIZE="$(awk -v b="${CURSOR_SIZE}" -v s="${CURSOR_DISPLAY_SCALE}" \
    'BEGIN { v = int(b * s + 0.5); if (v < 1) v = 24; print v }')"

# Prefer the user's and host's actual artwork. Rocky's older Adwaita theme has
# different cursor images even though its theme name is identical. Keep the
# container's own themes only as a fallback when the host lacks an asset.
CURSOR_PATH="${HOME}/.local/share/icons:${HOME}/.icons:/run/host/usr/local/share/icons:/run/host/usr/share/icons:/usr/share/icons"

ENV_FLAGS=(
    --env "XCURSOR_THEME=${CURSOR_THEME}"
    --env "XCURSOR_SIZE=${CURSOR_SIZE}"
    --env "XCURSOR_PATH=${CURSOR_PATH}"
)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The size of Resolve's own interface
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The long version is at the top of this file. The short version: XWayland tells
# every X11 program the screen is at 100%, whatever the screen is really at, so
# Resolve has to be told separately or it opens small on a 4K desktop.
#
# QT_AUTO_SCREEN_SCALE_FACTOR=0 is not optional. Left on, Qt asks XWayland how
# dense the screen is, gets "96 dpi" — the only answer XWayland ever gives — and
# quietly overrides the line below with 1.
QT_VARIABLE="$(conf_value qt_variable)"
[ -n "${QT_VARIABLE}" ] || QT_VARIABLE="${AQUARIUS_RESOLVE_QT_VAR:-scale-factor}"

# Menu readability is independent of overall scale. The config switch permits
# reverting to Resolve's own menus if a future app version changes its Qt ABI.
case "$(conf_value readable_menus)" in
    0|false|off) ENV_FLAGS+=(--env "AQUARIUS_RESOLVE_READABLE_MENUS=0") ;;
    *) ENV_FLAGS+=(--env "AQUARIUS_RESOLVE_READABLE_MENUS=1") ;;
esac

ENV_FLAGS+=(--env "QT_AUTO_SCREEN_SCALE_FACTOR=0")
case "${QT_VARIABLE}" in
    device-pixel-ratio)
        # Qt 5's older mechanism. Most Resolve advice on the internet names this
        # one, and it prefers whole numbers. Never set alongside the other:
        # Qt 5 reads both and they can multiply.
        ENV_FLAGS+=(--env "QT_DEVICE_PIXEL_RATIO=${SCALE}")
        ;;
    *)
        ENV_FLAGS+=(--env "QT_SCALE_FACTOR=${SCALE}")
        ;;
esac

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH FILE PICKER RESOLVE OPENS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added 2026-09-08, feature 008 item 5, as auto-detection rather than a bench
# experiment somebody has to remember to run.
#
# THE QUESTION. When Resolve opens a file dialog — Import, Save As, Render To —
# it draws Qt's own, which looks and behaves like nothing else on the computer:
# different sidebar, different shortcuts, no "Recent", no places you set up in
# Files. That is the loudest remaining seam between Resolve and the OS.
#
# Qt CAN be asked to use the desktop's shared picker instead — the same one
# Files, Firefox and the Aquarius Editor use — through xdg-desktop-portal on
# the session bus, which distrobox already shares (/run/user/<your id>). Qt does
# it by loading a "platform theme" plug-in called libqxdgdesktopportal.so.
#
# ⚠️ WHETHER THAT PLUG-IN EXISTS IS BLACKMAGIC'S DECISION, NOT OURS. Resolve
# carries its own Qt, built by Blackmagic, in /opt/resolve/libs. If they built
# the portal plug-in, it is in there and this works. If they did not — or if
# they built their dialogs with Qt's DontUseNativeDialog flag — then no
# operating system on earth can change it, and saying otherwise would be a lie
# in the documentation.
#
# So the OS LOOKS, every launch, and says which one you are getting. Nobody has
# to run an experiment; the answer is on the report line and in
# `aq resolve status`.
#
# THE OVERRIDE, for the bench: ~/.config/aquarius/resolve.conf
#     file_dialogs=own       never ask for the shared picker
#     file_dialogs=shared    ask for it whether or not the plug-in was found
#     file_dialogs=auto      look, and decide. The default.
PORTAL_PLUGIN="/opt/resolve/libs/plugins/platformthemes/libqxdgdesktopportal.so"

# One question, asked once per launch, because entering the container costs
# about a second and this script runs every time somebody clicks the icon.
portal_plugin_present() {
    [ -n "${AQ_RESOLVE_FAKE_PORTAL_PLUGIN+set}" ] && {
        [ "${AQ_RESOLVE_FAKE_PORTAL_PLUGIN}" = "yes" ]
        return
    }
    command -v distrobox > /dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
    command -v podman > /dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
    podman container exists "${CONTAINER}" 2> /dev/null || return 1
    distrobox enter --name "${CONTAINER}" -- test -f "${PORTAL_PLUGIN}" \
        > /dev/null 2>&1
}

FILE_DIALOGS="$(conf_value file_dialogs)"
[ -n "${FILE_DIALOGS}" ] || FILE_DIALOGS="auto"
case "${FILE_DIALOGS}" in
    shared) USE_PORTAL=1 ;;
    own) USE_PORTAL=0 ;;
    *)
        if portal_plugin_present; then USE_PORTAL=1; else USE_PORTAL=0; fi
        ;;
esac

if [ "${USE_PORTAL}" = "1" ]; then
    # Qt reads this name and loads the matching plug-in out of its own plug-in
    # folder. The D-Bus session it then talks to is the host's, because
    # distrobox shares /run/user/<your id> and the address that names it.
    ENV_FLAGS+=(--env "QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=xdgdesktopportal")
    DIALOG_WORDS="the desktop's shared picker"
else
    # Deliberately nothing. Setting the variable to a plug-in that is not there
    # makes Qt print a warning at every start and change nothing, which is the
    # worst of both.
    DIALOG_WORDS="Resolve's own"
fi

# Said out loud, because a launcher that silently changes how an application
# looks is a launcher nobody can debug. This goes to the terminal when started
# from one, and nowhere at all when started from an icon.
REPORT_LINE="aquarius-resolve-launch: interface at ${SCALE}x (${QT_VARIABLE}), pointer ${CURSOR_THEME} at ${CURSOR_SIZE}px, file dialogs: ${DIALOG_WORDS}"
echo "${REPORT_LINE}" >&2

# --report: that was the whole question. Printed on stdout this time, because
# something is reading it, and then we stop without starting anything.
if [ "${REPORT_ONLY}" = "1" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "${REPORT_LINE}"
    exit 0
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A FILE HANDED TO US BY FILES — "Open With → DaVinci Resolve"
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added 2026-09-08, feature 008 item 9.
#
# THE FAULT THIS FIXES. The app-menu entry ends in `%u`, which is the
# freedesktop way of saying "put the file the person chose here". So opening a
# project from Files reaches this script as an extra argument. Everything after
# this script's name is passed straight to aquarius-resolve-run INSIDE the
# container — and that script reads its FIRST argument as the program to run.
#
# So a file arriving on its own was read as a program, was not one, and Resolve
# never opened. "Open With → DaVinci Resolve" simply did nothing.
#
# THE RULE, and it is one line: an argument that begins /opt/resolve/ is a
# program inside the container (that is how the Blackmagic RAW Player and the
# control-panel setup tool are launched from the same machinery). Anything else
# is a FILE, and Resolve itself is named explicitly in front of it.
#
# AND file:// HAS TO BECOME A PATH. `%u` gives a URI, with spaces written as
# %20; Resolve is a 2016-era X11 application that wants a plain path. The
# conversion is deliberately narrow: strip `file://`, then turn each %XX back
# into its character. Backslashes are doubled first, so that a path containing
# one is not mangled by the same step.
uri_to_path() { # uri_to_path <argument>
    case "$1" in
        file://*)
            printf '%b' "$(printf '%s' "${1#file://}" \
                | sed -e 's/\\/\\\\/g' -e 's/%\([0-9A-Fa-f][0-9A-Fa-f]\)/\\x\1/g')"
            ;;
        *) printf '%s' "$1" ;;
    esac
}

RUN_ARGS=()
if [ "$#" -gt 0 ]; then
    case "$1" in
        /opt/resolve/*) : ;;                     # already names a program
        *) RUN_ARGS+=("/opt/resolve/bin/resolve") ;;
    esac
    for arg in "$@"; do
        RUN_ARGS+=("$(uri_to_path "${arg}")")
    done
else
    # No arguments at all: somebody clicked the DaVinci Resolve icon. Resolve
    # itself is named here rather than left to the container-side script's own
    # default, because the working folder worked out below has to be worked out
    # from SOMETHING, and that something is the name of the program.
    RUN_ARGS+=("/opt/resolve/bin/resolve")
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH FOLDER RESOLVE STARTS IN
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added 2026-09-09, and this is the fix for "clicking the DaVinci Resolve icon
# does nothing".
#
# THE FAULT. Every app-menu entry Blackmagic's installer writes carries a line
# saying which folder the program should be started from:
#
#     Path=/opt/resolve/                  (Resolve, Capture Logs, Panels, …)
#     Path=/opt/resolve/BlackmagicRAWPlayer/
#     Path=/opt/resolve/BlackmagicRAWSpeedTest
#
# `Path=` means "step into this folder FIRST, then run Exec". distrobox copied
# those lines out of the container and onto the entries in your apps — but
# /opt/resolve only exists INSIDE the container. GNOME tried to step into a
# folder that is not on this computer, gave up, and started nothing:
#
#     Failed to launch "DaVinci Resolve (on aquarius-resolve)":
#     Failed to change to directory "/opt/resolve/" (No such file or directory)
#
# No window, no error on screen, nothing. Starting the very same Exec line from
# a terminal worked, which is why it took a day to find.
#
# /usr/libexec/aquarius-resolve-entry now takes that line off the entries. This
# is the other half: the folder Blackmagic asked for is a real folder in the
# only place it makes sense — inside the container — so the stepping-in is done
# in there instead, right before the program starts.
#
# ⚠️ WHY IT MATTERS AND IS NOT JUST TIDINESS. Resolve's own logging settings ask
# for `./logs/rollinglog.txt` — a path RELATIVE to whatever folder Resolve was
# started from. Started from /opt/resolve that is /opt/resolve/logs, which is
# where Blackmagic put it and where their "capture logs" tool looks. Started
# from wherever the click happened to leave us, Resolve would quietly create a
# `logs` folder there — most likely straight in the home folder.
#
# THE FOLDERS, confirmed against the real container on 2026-09-09
# (`ls /opt/resolve`): the two Blackmagic RAW tools live in a folder of their
# own and want to start there; everything else — Resolve itself in /opt/resolve/bin,
# the control-panel setup, the log capture script, the uninstaller — wants
# /opt/resolve, which is what Blackmagic's own entries say.
resolve_work_folder() { # resolve_work_folder <program inside the container>
    case "$1" in
        /opt/resolve/BlackmagicRAWPlayer/*) printf '/opt/resolve/BlackmagicRAWPlayer' ;;
        /opt/resolve/BlackmagicRAWSpeedTest/*) printf '/opt/resolve/BlackmagicRAWSpeedTest' ;;
        *) printf '/opt/resolve' ;;
    esac
}

WORK_FOLDER="$(resolve_work_folder "${RUN_ARGS[0]}")"

# Remember the normal project window on the host. A singleton lock keeps repeated
# launches from starting competing watchers; RAW tools and --report never do this.
if [ "${RUN_ARGS[0]}" = /opt/resolve/bin/resolve ] && [ -x /usr/libexec/aquarius-resolve-window ]; then
    /usr/libexec/aquarius-resolve-window > /dev/null 2>&1 &
fi


# `distrobox enter --additional-flags` takes ONE string, so the flags are joined
# into one. None of these values can contain a space in practice — a cursor
# theme name is a folder name — and joining is what distrobox's own documented
# examples do.
EXTRA=""
for f in "${ENV_FLAGS[@]}"; do
    EXTRA="${EXTRA}${EXTRA:+ }${f}"
done

# ⚠️ "${RUN_ARGS[@]}" and not ${RUN_ARGS[@]}: every argument stays exactly one
# argument, so a drive called "Shoot 2026" or a project called "Ep 3 (final).drp"
# arrives whole rather than as three arguments Resolve cannot make sense of.
#
# ⚠️ AND THE `sh -c` WRAPPER IS HOW THE WORKING FOLDER GETS IN. Read it as a
# sentence: "inside the container, step into the first thing I hand you, throw
# that first thing away, then run aquarius-resolve-run with everything else."
#
#   * `sh` after the quoted script is not a mistake and not a duplicate — it is
#     the name the little script calls itself in any message it prints. It is
#     what makes the folder "$1" and the program "$2" onwards.
#   * every value still travels as its own separate argument, so a project
#     called "Ep 3 (final).drp" arrives whole, exactly as before.
#   * it is done HERE, on this computer, rather than inside the container's own
#     /usr/bin/aquarius-resolve-run — because that script lives in the runtime
#     container image, and changing it would mean rebuilding and republishing
#     that whole image. This costs nothing and needs no rebuild.
#   * if the folder somehow is not there, it says so and starts Resolve anyway.
#     A wrong folder is a misplaced log file; not starting at all is the fault
#     we are fixing.
#
# The single quotes around that little script are deliberate and shellcheck is
# told so: `$1`, `$@` and `$PWD` belong to the shell INSIDE the container, and
# must reach it as those three characters rather than being replaced here.
# shellcheck disable=SC2016
exec distrobox enter --name "${CONTAINER}" --additional-flags "${EXTRA}" \
    -- /bin/sh -c '
        cd "$1" 2> /dev/null || printf "%s\n" \
            "aquarius-resolve: $1 is not in this container; starting from $PWD instead." >&2
        shift
        # Repair the absolute opener Resolve uses for Login via web. This
        # runs inside the mutable container; a failure must not stop editing.
        if ! /bin/sh /run/host/usr/libexec/aquarius-resolve-browser/setup; then
            printf "%s\n" "Resolve browser integration could not be prepared." >&2
            /usr/bin/distrobox-host-exec /usr/bin/notify-send --app-name="DaVinci Resolve" \
                -- "Browser integration needs attention" \
                "Resolve can still open. Restart it after the OS update to retry browser setup." \
                >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
        fi
        if [ -x /run/host/usr/libexec/aquarius-resolve-menu-run ]; then
            exec /usr/bin/aquarius-resolve-run /run/host/usr/libexec/aquarius-resolve-menu-run "$@"
        fi
        exec /usr/bin/aquarius-resolve-run "$@"
    ' sh "${WORK_FOLDER}" "${RUN_ARGS[@]}"
